  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 210 PM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nStratus has been persistent across the northern half of southwest Kansas \nin the post cold front environment, holding temperatures down\nsome through midday, though where clouds have erroded temps have\nwarmed quickly. The stationary frontal boundary was very \nclearly outlined by surface observations at midday, stradding \nthe Kansas/Oklahoma border. August sunshine will continue to \nerode the stratus through the remainder of the afternoon.\n\nTonight, the stationary front will lift very slowly back\nnorthward as a warm front into southwest Kansas. Moistening at\nlow and mid levels will result in elevated instability and scattered\nthunderstorm development. In generall, rainfall amounts of \n0.10-0.25\" will be more common, though isolated amounts closer \nto 0.50\" or slightly higher would be possible in the stronger \nstorms. Precip chances will be highest along and north of the \ndeveloping mid level frontogenetic region overnight, getting as \nhigh as 50-60% in those areas. \n\nThursday the long wave trough axis will shift slightly eastward\nallowing for modest height rises across the southern Rockies and\ninto far southwest KS. This will create a stronger flow regime\nover western KS during the day and will allow for decent deep\nlayer shear and with increasing low level moisture, modest\ninstability through the day. With the building upper ridge to\nthe west, differential radiational cooling will allow for the\ndevelopment of a nocturnal low level jet which should again lead\nsome potential for thunderstorms development overnight Thursday\ninto Friday, however coverage will be more limited.\n\nFriday and beyond...an expanding southwestern U.S. upper ridge\nwill signal a much drier period with large scale subsidence\nprevailing. This will also lead to afternoon highs around the\n100 degree mark and slightly above for the weekend into the\nfirst of next week.",
